WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.952
Not every possible duplicate should be automatically merged.

00:00:03.952 --> 00:00:11.347
This query compares customer records in pairs and looks for rows that share the same normalized phone number.

00:00:11.347 --> 00:00:17.742
It uses a self join, meaning the customers table is joined to another copy of itself.

00:00:17.742 --> 00:00:24.812
The condition that the left identifier must be smaller than the right identifier prevents us from seeing the same pair

00:00:24.812 --> 00:00:25.521
twice.

00:00:25.975 --> 00:00:34.002
If two records share a phone number, the query returns their identifiers, names, and emails for comparison.

00:00:34.002 --> 00:00:37.462
Suppose two customers have the same phone number but different names and emails.

00:00:37.462 --> 00:00:46.122
That could represent a duplicate, but it could also represent two people in the same household.

00:00:46.122 --> 00:00:52.747
This is exactly the kind of situation where SQL should identify a candidate and a person may need to make the final

00:00:52.747 --> 00:00:53.877
decision.
